<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="brooksgroves.com pikas part 2" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
<metadata><name>Washington's missing pika sites (pre-1950, no GBIF record within 3 km since 2000)</name><link href="https://brooksgroves.com/blog/pikas-part-2-washington.html"><text>Pikas, part 2</text></link></metadata>
<wpt lat="48.96670" lon="-121.23330"><name>Glacier Peak</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. Pinned about 95 km north of the volcano, on Mount Spickard, which was called Glacier Peak until 1963 (part 2). Either mountain is possible; the label doesn't say. Getting there: Remote either way. Worth a museum check of the original label.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.84800" lon="-121.69000"><name>Mt. Baker: Austin Pass and the lodge</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. Five specimens labelled Mt. Baker, Mt. Baker Lodge, Austin Pass and Mt. Baker Lookout share one pin about 12 km south-west of Austin Pass. At Austin Pass itself, by the Heather Meadows road, GBIF has 61 pika records since 2000 within 3 km. Pikas are doing fine here. Getting there: Artist Point road, Heather Meadows. Easy.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.81020" lon="-117.13020"><name>Round Top Mountain, Selkirks</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. Georeferenced in 2003 to a 4,465-ft Round Top in Spokane County, a record marked unverified. Dalquest's own book puts his Round Top in Pend Oreille County at about 6,000 ft, and there is a 6,453-ft Round Top Mountain in the Selkirks, 105 km north. A GBIF record from 2023 sits 1.4 km from that summit, and someone photographed a pika on the Shedroof Divide Trail there in September 2026. Getting there: Shedroof Divide Trail, Colville National Forest. A long day.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.74930" lon="-121.12070"><name>Tye (old Wellington)</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. Pinned where the Tye River meets the Foss, at 341 m. In 1939 “Tye” was the railroad town below Stevens Pass that had been called Wellington until the 1910 avalanche, 14 km east and at 3,257 ft. GBIF has a pika record from 2026 about 300 m from the old townsite. Getting there: Wellington ghost town and the Iron Goat Trail, off US 2. Easy.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="46.92500" lon="-121.82500"><name>Mt. Rainier: Spray Park and Lake James</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. The four 1919 specimens are pinned to Rainier's summit, where no pika lives (part 1 used the collector's label elevations instead). The labels name Spray Park and Lake James, and both have pikas on record since 2000 within 3 km. Getting there: Spray Park trail from Mowich Lake. Busy, beautiful, pikas in the talus.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="46.20260" lon="-121.49060"><name>Mt. Adams (summit pin)</name><desc>Pinned in the wrong place. Labelled just “Mt. Adams” and pinned to the summit at 3,738 m. The label doesn't say where on the mountain it was taken. Pikas are recorded on the south-east side today, around Bird Creek Meadows. Getting there: No way to know where; Bird Creek Meadows is the nearest known colony.</desc><type>Pinned in the wrong place</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.93000" lon="-119.97000"><name>Windy Peak, Pasayten</name><desc>Just past the circle. Two records from 2022, 3.9 and 4.1 km away and a few hundred metres lower than the summit pin. Pikas are almost certainly still on this mountain; nobody has posted one from the top. Getting there: Pasayten Wilderness. A long trail day.</desc><type>Just past the circle</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.89370" lon="-121.66840"><name>Bagley Creek, Mt. Herman</name><desc>Just past the circle. Right next door to the busiest pika country in the state. GBIF has 16 records since 2000 within 5 km and 89 within 10, around Bagley Lakes and Heather Meadows; iNaturalist has one within 3 km. Getting there: Bagley Lakes loop, Heather Meadows. Easy.</desc><type>Just past the circle</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.24330" lon="-120.55920"><name>Table Mountain, Wenatchee NF</name><desc>Just past the circle. Records from 2022 sit 4.8 to 5 km away. Close enough that this one is probably about where people walk, not about the pikas. Getting there: Forest roads north of Ellensburg, around Haney Meadow.</desc><type>Just past the circle</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.08690" lon="-121.37920"><name>Naches Pass</name><desc>Just past the circle. The nearest GBIF record since 2000 is 5.9 km away, and iNaturalist has 14 pika observations within 10 km. The old wagon-road pass gets few visitors compared with Chinook Pass next door. Getting there: The old wagon route over the crest. Rough forest roads.</desc><type>Just past the circle</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="46.15110" lon="-121.44640"><name>Bird Creek Meadows, Mt. Adams</name><desc>Just past the circle. GBIF's nearest record since 2000 is 3.6 km away, just past the circle, but iNaturalist has five pika observations within 3 km, including research-grade photos from 2018, 2023 and 2024 about 2 km west of the pin. Re-found. Getting there: Bird Creek Meadows, Yakama Nation Tract D (check access and fees).</desc><type>Just past the circle</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.82000" lon="-119.63000"><name>Loomis</name><desc>The low sites. Labelled with a town at 397 m in the Okanogan. Collectors often wrote the nearest post office, so this pika probably came from the hills around it, maybe the high country to the west. The nearest record since 2000 is 26 km away. Getting there: Okanogan highlands west of Loomis; Chopaka and the Pasayten edge.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.30930" lon="-120.65650"><name>Stehekin</name><desc>The low sites. Labelled with the village at the head of Lake Chelan, 371 m. The animal probably came from the talus somewhere above. No pika has been posted within 10 km, but Stehekin is reached only by boat, plane or a long hike. Getting there: Talus along the Stehekin valley road and Rainbow Loop.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.90890" lon="-120.83790"><name>Twin Lakes (near Lake Wenatchee)</name><desc>The low sites. The pin is at 871 m. The nearest record since 2000 is 4.5 km away but about 900 m higher, up on the ridges. Getting there: Lake Wenatchee area. Which Twin Lakes is uncertain.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.31670" lon="-121.33330"><name>Lake Keechelus</name><desc>The low sites. The type locality: the specimen that named the Cascade pika subspecies, Ochotona princeps brunnescens, was taken here by George Cantwell on 23 August 1917. Today Interstate 90 runs along the lake. The site sits at about 765 m, and the nearest record since 2000 is 6 km away and about 500 m higher, though pikas are everywhere around Snoqualmie Pass. Getting there: Talus along the lake and I-90 corridor. Stay safe off the freeway.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="46.98870" lon="-121.09470"><name>Goose Prairie, Bumping River</name><desc>The low sites. At 778 m on the dry east side of the Cascades. The nearest record since 2000 is 6.2 km away and 300 m higher, and there's only one within 10 km. Getting there: Bumping River road, west of SR 410.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="46.98140" lon="-121.09040"><name>Milk Creek, Naches Highway</name><desc>The low sites. Dalquest singled this place out: Milk Creek was the driest, warmest life zone where he found pikas in Washington. At roughly 800 m, it's exactly the kind of low, warm site that part 1 found missing most often. Nothing on record within 3 km since 2000. Getting there: SR 410 corridor near Milk Creek. Rock slides by the highway.</desc><type>The low sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.87550" lon="-121.36370"><name>Whatcom Pass</name><desc>Real gaps: go look. Deep in North Cascades National Park, a multi-day trip each way. One pika record within 10 km, from 2014. Probably a gap in hikers more than in pikas, but nobody knows. Getting there: Whatcom Pass via Hannegan Pass and the Chilliwack. Backpack.</desc><type>Real gaps: go look</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.60750" lon="-118.47840"><name>Sherman Pass, Kettle River Range</name><desc>Real gaps: go look. The biggest gap on the map. Dalquest took young pikas of several sizes here on 11 September 1938, so it was a breeding colony. Since 2000 there's no GBIF pika record within 82 km and no iNaturalist observation of any grade within 10 km. Sherman Pass is on Highway 20 and the Kettle Crest Trail starts right there. Getting there: Kettle Crest Trail from Sherman Pass, SR 20. Easy access; listen for the eep.</desc><type>Real gaps: go look</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.59970" lon="-119.74450"><name>Funk Mountain, Okanogan</name><desc>Real gaps: go look. No pika on record within 10 km since 2000, on GBIF or iNaturalist. The nearest is 14 km away. There's a fire lookout on Funk Mountain, so people do go up. Getting there: Funk Mountain lookout, north-west of Conconully.</desc><type>Real gaps: go look</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.31470" lon="-121.76110"><name>Mount Higgins lookout</name><desc>Real gaps: go look. Taken at the old lookout station above the North Fork Stillaguamish. Nothing within 10 km since 2000; the nearest GBIF record is 13.6 km away. Getting there: Mount Higgins trail off SR 530. Steep.</desc><type>Real gaps: go look</type></wpt>
</gpx>